      ":question/id": "f5634580-64c8-5ae9-a992-28134bd95405",
      ":question/problem": "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{8\\sqrt{1-64{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n{{0}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $64{x}^{2}=(8x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=8x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{8}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{8\\sqrt{1-64{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{8}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(8x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{8}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{64}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{64}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{64}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(8x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
